  "honest_notes": "CLEAN RESULT - stated plainly because it is the finding: across 49 institutional PDFs (30 Google-related, 19 Microsoft-related) the mojibake regex found ZERO matches (total mojibake across corpus = 0). Verified with positive controls (the regex fires correctly on synthetic double-encoded strings) and with deeper probes: every raw U+00C3/U+00C2 hit in the corpus turned out to be LEGITIMATE uppercase Portuguese (CONTRATAÇÃO, GOIÂNIA, MANHÃ S.A., MAGALHÃES, PREÂMBULO), and the same files contain thousands of correctly-encoded lowercase accents (ç ã é õ: up to 7,851 in a single AGEHAB tender) - so this is genuinely clean encoding, not a regex artifact. Google's own pipelines (policies.google.com -> gstatic PDF exports, producer Skia/PDF m142-m155, i.e. Chrome print stack) are perfect in ALL 18 locale variants incl. pt-BR/es/fr/de/pl/ro/tr/ja; Microsoft's own licensing/GSA PDFs likewise. Third-party pipelines around the two companies are also clean: EC antitrust/merger decisions (EN), EUR-Lex Official Journal multilingual PT/ES/FR versions, SEC EDGAR courtesy PDFs (Alphabet ARS 2025/2026, Microsoft DEF 14A 2024/2025), US state procurement docs, .edu contract templates, and even Brazilian Word-2016/SEI Gazette PDFs that we probed at the byte-pair level. Contrast with the LATAM Airlines corpus: mojibake there came from legacy print/OCR/HTML conversion stacks; none of the pipelines generating or hosting Google/Microsoft institutional documents in 2023-2026 show UTF-8-as-Latin-1 double-encoding at all. The 3 worst_examples are the files with the HIGHEST volume of C3/C2-pair byte sequences that a naive pipeline could corrupt (all verified legitimate accents, mojibake_count=0). Caveats: (1) ~8% of EC decision pages are image-only annexes with no text layer - a text-layer mojibake test cannot apply to them (no OCR artifacts either since no OCR text); (2) EUR-Lex TXT/PDF endpoints are bot-gated (HTTP 202 async queue) - the 4 OJ PDFs were retrieved via browser session polling, all URLs recorded for re-verification; (3) edu-uca Google Apps agreement is a 2016 template (kept for contract-template coverage; the rest skew 2023-2026); (4) 4 redundant gstatic locale duplicates were dropped to honor the max-30 per company cap; (5) privacy rule: no personal/consumer documents - only institutional terms, contracts, tenders, regulatory decisions; Brazilian docs mention public officials and reseller companies in their official capacity; (6) br-sicon contract was classified 'microsoft' after text extraction showed its object is Microsoft licensing products."
